the word sorry isn't enough for  survivors and those bereaved  spread right across ireland the secrets  of 18 institutions concealed the deaths  of an estimated 9 000 babies and young  children  hidden mass graves one in the corner  here by a playground  this is tune county galway it was a home  run by  nuns where nearly 800 babies were buried  in and around a disused sewage tank  tumor nine more mother and baby homes  run by the catholic church  took in young unmarried mothers there  were eight  other institutions the state was  supposed to be paying for care  yet the women lived like prisoners most  survivors and bereave relatives believe  the reports findings and government  action  is too little too late it seems that our  worst fears have been realized  they are going to trivialize the major  overarching topics that relate to the  growth human rights abuses in those  homes  birth outside marriage had been treated  not only as a sin  but a crime in a country where a  staunchly conservative catholic  church held considerable sway  the penalty for young women was  servitude often  sexual abuse forced labor and loss of  their babies  thousands of infants died through  neglect cruelty and  other abuses or they were sold by nuns  in what they considered to be adoption  this was my mother bridges and this was  my father with him  anna corrigan's mother bridget dolan was  in tune  a book has been written about her she  lost one son who died of neglect  another was taken away by the nuns never  to be seen or heard of again  it's the culmination of a lot of years  of waiting and  research she says it was a series of  government cover-ups and deliberate  delays and denials  our mantra was delayed and i till we all  go away and die  my parents are gone my brothers are  missing  dead not dead my aunt is stumped in a  pit  in la grey and i'm the voice  anna's voice has been a loud one in  calling for justice and closure  she says the report falls short of both  no one has been brought to justice  yet it went on for 76 years ending in  1998.  the report says the church and state  were partly responsible  but they provided refuge the women  weren't forced into the homes according  to the report  it says the fathers of their children  and their families provided  no refuge that assertion has been met  with outrage  what happened has often been termed the  irish mother and babies scandal  many may feel that's an understatement  and the word  atrocities may prove more accurate  andrew simmons al jazeera
 

